import TestimonyArguments.SolaFide.Dispute

/-!
# TestimonyArguments.SolaFide.Reformation — the Reformation's case, 1521–1546

Luther did not argue alone. In the twenty-five years between his first quarrel
with Rome and the Council of Trent's decree on justification in January 1547,
the men around him wrote the doctrine down again and again, in Latin, German and
English, and Catholic theologians answered them in print. This chapter hears
thirteen of those voices, on both sides, in the order they wrote, so that no
reply comes before what it answers. Each is quoted from a printed edition that
anyone can read in a public scan.

**Melanchthon, 1521.** Philipp Melanchthon, Luther's colleague at Wittenberg,
published the first Protestant handbook of doctrine, the *Loci communes*, in
December 1521. "Nothing of our works, however good they seem or are, is
righteousness," he wrote, "but faith alone in the mercy and grace of God in
Jesus Christ is righteousness", and, a few pages on, "therefore faith alone
justifies" (*Justificat igitur sola fides*). He argued from Abraham, who
"believed the LORD, and He counted it to him as righteousness" (Genesis 15:6),
and from Romans 4:5, where faith is counted to the one "who does not work but
believes".

**Zwingli and Eck, 1525.** At Zurich, Huldrych Zwingli printed his *Commentary
on True and False Religion* in March 1525. Paul, he wrote, "says that Christ is
of no use to us if righteousness come from our works, Gal. 2:16; and that grace
through Christ is of no avail if salvation be due to works, Rom. 4:4 and 11:6".
The same year Johann Eck, Luther's opponent at Leipzig in 1519, published his
*Enchiridion*, a handbook of answers to the Lutherans that went through many
printings. Its chapter on faith and works opens with the thesis that "faith does
not suffice without works", and that works, by "the grace of God accepting
them", are "meritorious of eternal life". Against the Lutheran reading of "the
righteous shall live by faith" he says that the heretic who adds "alone" "tears
and falsifies the text, since it nowhere says that the righteous lives by faith
alone", and he sets James 2:24 beside it: "a man is justified by works, and not
by faith only".

**Tyndale, 1528.** In England the doctrine was a heresy to be listed, and
Tyndale wrote from exile. *The Parable of the Wicked Mammon*, printed at Marburg
in May 1528, opens: "That faith only before all works and without all merits,
but Christ's only, justifieth and setteth us at peace with God, is proved by
Paul in the first chapter to the Romans". He met James directly. "Faith
therefore justifieth in the heart, and before God; and the deeds justify
outwardly before the world, that is, testify only before men, what we are
inwardly before God."

**Augsburg, 1530–1531.** On June 25, 1530, the Lutheran princes presented their
confession to the Emperor at Augsburg. Its fourth article teaches that people
"cannot be justified before God by their own strength, merits, or works, but are
freely justified for Christ's sake, through faith", citing Romans 3 and 4, and
its sixth quotes a father, then thought to be Ambrose: "he who believes in
Christ is saved, freely receiving remission of sins, without works, by faith
alone." On August 3 the Catholic theologians' answer, the Confutation, was read
to the Diet. It calls the Lutherans' "ascription of justification to faith
alone" "diametrically opposite the truth of the Gospel", and from 1 Corinthians
13:2, "though I have all faith … and have not charity, I am nothing", it says
that "St. Paul certifies to the princes and the entire Church that faith alone
does not justify." Faith is approved, "not faith alone, which some incorrectly
teach, but faith which worketh by love" (Galatians 5:6). Melanchthon's *Apology*
answered in 1531: "The particle alone offends some, although even Paul says,
Rom. 3, 28", and "therefore by faith alone we are justified." Of James 2:24 he
wrote that "if the adversaries do not attach their own opinions concerning the
merits of works, the words of James have in them nothing that is of
disadvantage."

**Barnes, about 1531.** Robert Barnes, an Augustinian friar turned Lutheran, put
an article titled "Onely fayth iustifieth before God" into his *Supplication* to
Henry VIII. He argued from Paul at Antioch, "by hym are all that beleue,
iustified from all thynges" (Acts 13:39), and from the fathers: Ambrose on
Romans 3, "they doing nothing, nor nothing deseruing, alonely by fayth are
iustified by the gift of God".

**Cajetan, 1532, and Luther, 1535.** Cardinal Cajetan, who had examined Luther at
Augsburg in 1518, wrote at Rome in May 1532 that "the common teaching of the
church is most true, that the remission of sins comes not through unformed faith
but through faith formed by charity". That is the schoolmen's distinction: a
faith that only believes is "unformed", and it becomes the faith that justifies
when love (*caritas*) gives it its form and life (*fides caritate formata*). Of
Galatians 5:6, he said, Paul "clearly ascribes power in Christ not to faith of
any kind but to faith working through love." Luther, lecturing on Galatians in
1535, named that teaching as the corruption Paul fought. On 2:4–5, where Paul
would not yield "so that the truth of the gospel would remain", he said: "the
truth of the gospel is, that our righteousness cometh by faith alone, without
the works of the law. The corruption, or falsehood of the gospel is, that we are
justified by faith, but not without the works of the law. … Even so do our
Papists at this day. For they say, that we must believe in Christ, and that
faith is the foundation of our salvation: but it justifieth not, except it be
furnished with charity."

**Sadoleto and Calvin, 1539.** In March 1539 Cardinal Jacopo Sadoleto wrote to
the people of Geneva, whom the Reformers had won, to call them back. He used the
Reformers' own words in his own sense: "we obtain this blessing of complete and
perpetual salvation by faith alone in God and in Jesus Christ", but "faith is a
term of full and ample signification", which includes "the hope and desire of
obeying God, together with love", and "in this very faith love is essentially
comprehended as the chief and primary cause of our salvation." He cited Titus
2:14, a people "zealous of good works". John Calvin answered in September: "But
faith, you say, is a general term, and has a larger signification. I answer,
that Paul, whenever he attributes to it the power of justifying, at the same
time restricts it to a gratuitous promise of the divine favour." From Romans
4:6–8, the blessing of the one to whom God counts righteousness apart from
works, he concluded: "We deny that good works have any share in justification,
but we claim full authority for them in the lives of the righteous."

**Regensburg, 1541.** The last attempt at agreement came at the colloquy of
Regensburg in May 1541, where Melanchthon and Martin Bucer sat with Eck,
Johannes Gropper and the papal legate, Cardinal Gasparo Contarini. Their fifth
article, on justification, was accepted by the speakers of both sides. It says
that "the sinner is justified through a living and efficacious faith"; that "the
justifying faith is that faith which is efficacious through love; but meanwhile
this is true, that we are justified by this faith in so far as it lays hold of
the mercy and righteousness which is imputed to us for Christ's sake"; and that
the believer, though he receives an inherent righteousness too, "does not rest
on" it, "but on the righteousness of Christ alone, given to us." Contarini's
letter of May 25 explains it as a "double righteousness", one inherent and one
given with Christ, and reads "efficacious through love" as "formed by charity".
He grounds the resting on Christ's righteousness in Philippians 3:8–9, "not
having a righteousness of my own".

**What the debate decides.** Every step in it is rated `disputed`, because
each side grants the other's texts and denies its conclusion: the Confutation
and Eck cite Romans 3 and Galatians, the *Apology* and Tyndale take up James 2,
and Luther and Calvin read Galatians 5:6 and the texts on reward. So each attack
across the line is met by one in return, and nothing is forced
(`luthers_contemporaries_force_nothing`). The Reformers' positions can all be held
together (`the_reformers_stand_together`), and so can the Catholic positions,
Regensburg among them, because the article's faith "efficacious through love" is
read here as Contarini read it, faith formed by love
(`rome_and_regensburg_stand_together`). The two sides part at two claims: whether
the faith that justifies does so alone or as formed by love, and whether good
works, made worthy by grace, are a ground of salvation. Trent took up both in
1547.
-/

namespace TestimonyArguments.SolaFide

open Testimony Testimony.Bib Testimony.Logic Testimony.Logic.Framework Testimony.Scripture

/-! ### The texts both sides cited -/

/-- A text both sides of the Reformation accepted, cited to Scripture alone. -/
def reformationText (label : String) (refs : List ScriptureCitation) : AtomMeta :=
  { label := label
  , kind := .textual
  , source :=
      { primary := .scripture refs
      , tradition := .christianHistoricalGrammatical
      , confidence := .consensus } }

/-- The sources of the Reformation's debate: Scripture alone for each text the
voices cite, so that no later reader is heard on them, and for every other
claim the sources the argument gives it elsewhere. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def reformationCite : Claim → AtomMeta
  | .romans3_28 =>
    reformationText "Romans 3:28: a person is justified by faith apart from works of the law"
      [{ ref := .verse ⟨.romans, 3, 28⟩ }]
  | .romans4_4_5 =>
    reformationText "Romans 4:4–5: to the one who does not work but believes, faith is counted"
      [{ ref := .range ⟨.romans, 4, 4, 4, 5⟩ }]
  | .ephesians2_8_9 =>
    reformationText "Ephesians 2:8–9: saved by grace through faith, not of works"
      [{ ref := .range ⟨.ephesians, 2, 8, 2, 9⟩ }]
  | .galatians2_16 =>
    reformationText "Galatians 2:16: no one is justified by works of the law"
      [{ ref := .verse ⟨.galatians, 2, 16⟩ }]
  | .galatians1_6_9 =>
    reformationText "Galatians 1:6–9: a different gospel is no gospel, and is accursed"
      [{ ref := .range ⟨.galatians, 1, 6, 1, 9⟩ }]
  | .gen15_6AbramBelieved =>
    reformationText "Genesis 15:6: Abram believed the LORD, and it was counted as righteousness"
      [{ ref := .verse ⟨.genesis, 15, 6⟩ }]
  | .james2FaithWithoutWorksIsDead =>
    reformationText "James 2:14–26: faith without works is dead"
      [{ ref := .range ⟨.james, 2, 14, 2, 26⟩ }]
  | .james2JustifiedByWorks =>
    reformationText "James 2:21–25: Abraham and Rahab were justified by works"
      [{ ref := .range ⟨.james, 2, 21, 2, 25⟩ }]
  | .romans4_6_8 =>
    reformationText "Romans 4:6–8: blessed is the one counted righteous apart from works"
      [{ ref := .range ⟨.romans, 4, 6, 4, 8⟩ }]
  | .rewardTextsPromiseReward =>
    reformationText "1 Cor 15:58; Heb 6:10; 10:35; 2 Tim 4:8: God rewards the labor of believers"
      [ { ref := .verse ⟨.firstCorinthians, 15, 58⟩ }, { ref := .verse ⟨.hebrews, 6, 10⟩ }
      , { ref := .verse ⟨.hebrews, 10, 35⟩ }, { ref := .verse ⟨.secondTimothy, 4, 8⟩ } ]
  | c => baseCite c

/-! ### Melanchthon's *Loci*, 1521 -/

/-- **Who draws the step, in 1521.** Melanchthon, in the *Loci communes*,
pp. 164 and 168 of Plitt and Kolde's edition. Rated `disputed`, because the
Confutation of 1530 grants Paul's texts, citing Romans 3 itself, and denies that
faith alone justifies. -/
def melanchthonLociStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work melanchthonLoci (.page 168)
  , supporting := [.work melanchthonLoci (.page 164)]
  , tradition := .reformedProtestant
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Faith alone justifies, as Melanchthon read Paul in 1521.** Abram "believed
the LORD, and He counted it to him as righteousness" (Genesis 15:6), and Paul
says that to the one "who does not work but believes in Him who justifies the
ungodly, his faith is counted as righteousness" (Romans 4:5). So "nothing of our
works … is righteousness, but faith alone", and "therefore faith alone
justifies". -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def melanchthonLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Melanchthon: faith alone justifies (Loci, 1521)"
  , grounds := [p .romans4_4_5, p .gen15_6AbramBelieved]
  , step := ⋀ [p .romans4_4_5, p .gen15_6AbramBelieved] ➝ p .justificationByFaithAlone
  , delivers := p .justificationByFaithAlone
  , inference := some melanchthonLociStep }

/-- Melanchthon's *Loci* of 1521, as a position. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def melanchthonCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  melanchthonLine.asPackage reformationCite "justification is by faith alone"

/-! ### Zwingli's *Commentary*, 1525 -/

/-- **Who draws the step, in 1525.** Zwingli, in the *Commentary on True and
False Religion*, p. 137 of Heller's English. Rated `disputed`, because the
Confutation grants the texts and holds that "it is entirely contrary to Holy
Scripture to deny that our works are meritorious" (Jacobs, p. 211). -/
def zwingliStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work zwingliTrueAndFalseReligion (.page 137)
  , tradition := .reformedProtestant
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Grace avails nothing if salvation is due to works, as Zwingli read Paul in
1525.** No one is justified by works of the law (Galatians 2:16); to the worker
wages are owed, not a gift (Romans 4:4); and if it is by grace, it is no longer
by works (Romans 11:6). Zwingli reads "works" the same way in both verses, as
the argument needs. So no work is a ground of salvation: believers "through
faith … are sure of salvation", their sins "not imputed to them as such because
of their faith". -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def zwingliLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Zwingli: grace avails nothing if salvation is due to works (1525)"
  , grounds :=
      [p .galatians2_16, p .romans4_4_5, p .romans11_6, p .worksKeepsItsSenseInRomans4And11]
  , step :=
      ⋀ [p .galatians2_16, p .romans4_4_5, p .romans11_6, p .worksKeepsItsSenseInRomans4And11] ➝
        p .salvationNotByWorks
  , delivers := p .salvationNotByWorks
  , inference := some zwingliStep }

/-- Zwingli's *Commentary* of 1525, as a position. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def zwingliCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  zwingliLine.asPackage reformationCite "salvation is not by works"

/-! ### Eck's *Enchiridion*, 1525 -/

/-- **Who draws the step against faith alone, in 1525.** Eck, in the
*Enchiridion*, fols. 45r–51r of the 1536 printing. Rated `disputed`, because
Melanchthon's *Apology* grants James 2 and Galatians 5:6 and denies that they
exclude faith alone. -/
def eckStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work eckEnchiridion (.sectionRef "fols. 50v–51r")
  , supporting := [.work eckEnchiridion (.sectionRef "fols. 45r, 48v")]
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Faith does not suffice without works, as Eck read Scripture in 1525.**
Faith without works is dead (James 2:17), Abraham was justified by works, and "a
man is justified by works, and not by faith only" (2:21–24); what counts in
Christ is "faith working through love" (Galatians 5:6). And "the righteous shall
live by faith" nowhere says "by faith alone". So justification is not by faith
alone. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def eckLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Eck: faith does not suffice without works (Enchiridion, 1525)"
  , grounds := [p .james2FaithWithoutWorksIsDead, p .james2JustifiedByWorks, p .galatians5_6]
  , step := ⋀ [p .james2FaithWithoutWorksIsDead, p .james2JustifiedByWorks, p .galatians5_6] ➝
      notP .justificationByFaithAlone
  , delivers := notP .justificationByFaithAlone
  , inference := some eckStep }

/-- **Who draws the step from reward to merit, in 1525 and 1530.** Eck, in his
first proposition, and the Confutation, answering the Augsburg Confession's
fourth article. Rated `disputed`, because the Reformed grant that God rewards
the good works of believers and deny that the works merit it (Westminster
Confession, XVI.6). -/
def meritStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work romanConfutation (.page 211)
  , supporting := [.work eckEnchiridion (.sectionRef "fol. 45r")]
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Works made worthy by grace are meritorious, as Rome answered in 1525 and
1530.** God promises to reward the labour of believers: "the crown of
righteousness, which the Lord, the righteous judge, will award to me" (2 Timothy
4:8, with 1 Corinthians 15:58 and Hebrews 6:10 and 10:35). Eck put it in his
first proposition, that works are "meritorious of eternal life, by the grace of
God accepting them", and the Confutation answered the Augsburg Confession that
"it is entirely contrary to Holy Scripture to deny that our works are
meritorious", while all Catholics "confess that our works of themselves have no
merit, but that God's grace makes them worthy of eternal life". So good works,
made worthy by grace, are a ground of salvation. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def meritLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Rome: works made worthy by grace are meritorious (1525, 1530)"
  , grounds := [p .rewardTextsPromiseReward]
  , step := p .rewardTextsPromiseReward ➝ notP .salvationNotByWorks
  , delivers := notP .salvationNotByWorks
  , inference := some meritStep }

/-- Eck's *Enchiridion*, as a position: faith does not suffice without works,
and works made worthy by grace merit eternal life. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def eckCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  { name := "Eck, Enchiridion (1525)"
  , cite := reformationCite
  , premises := caseOf [eckLine, meritLine] [] []
  , conclusion := notP .justificationByFaithAlone
  , conclusionLabel := "justification is not by faith alone"
  , inferences := [eckStep, meritStep] }

/-! ### Tyndale's *Wicked Mammon*, 1528 -/

/-- **Who draws the step to faith only, in 1528.** Tyndale, in *The Parable of
the Wicked Mammon*, pp. 46–47 of Walter's edition. Rated `disputed`, as
Melanchthon's is, because Eck and the Confutation grant Paul and deny the
conclusion. -/
def tyndaleStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work tyndaleWickedMammon (.pages 46 47)
  , tradition := .reformedProtestant
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Who reads James that way, in 1528.** Tyndale, pp. 61 and 119. Rated
`disputed`, because Eck grants James 2 and reads it the other way, as excluding
faith alone. -/
def tyndaleJamesStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work tyndaleWickedMammon (.page 119)
  , supporting := [.work tyndaleWickedMammon (.page 61)]
  , tradition := .reformedProtestant
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Faith only justifies, as Tyndale read Paul in 1528.** "I am not ashamed of
the gospel", and in it "the just or righteous must live by faith" (Romans
1:16–17, as Tyndale quotes it). So "faith only before all works and without all
merits, but Christ's only, justifieth and setteth us at peace with God". -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def tyndaleLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Tyndale: faith only justifieth (Wicked Mammon, 1528)"
  , grounds := [p .romans1_16_17]
  , step := p .romans1_16_17 ➝ p .justificationByFaithAlone
  , delivers := p .justificationByFaithAlone
  , inference := some tyndaleStep }

/-- **Deeds justify before the world, as Tyndale read James in 1528.** James
says that faith without works is dead, and that Abraham was justified by works
when he offered Isaac (2:17, 2:21). Tyndale reads the deed as a witness: "His
deed justified him before the world; that is, it declared and uttered the faith
which both justified him before God". So James and Paul agree, because "faith
therefore justifieth in the heart, and before God; and the deeds justify
outwardly before the world." -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def tyndaleJamesLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Tyndale: deeds justify outwardly before the world (Wicked Mammon, 1528)"
  , grounds := [p .james2FaithWithoutWorksIsDead, p .james2JustifiedByWorks]
  , step := ⋀ [p .james2FaithWithoutWorksIsDead, p .james2JustifiedByWorks] ➝
      p .james2_24Compatible
  , delivers := p .james2_24Compatible
  , inference := some tyndaleJamesStep }

/-- Tyndale's *Wicked Mammon*, as a position: faith only justifies before God,
and the works of James 2 justify before the world. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def tyndaleCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  { name := "Tyndale, The Parable of the Wicked Mammon (1528)"
  , cite := reformationCite
  , premises := caseOf [tyndaleLine, tyndaleJamesLine] [] []
  , conclusion := p .justificationByFaithAlone
  , conclusionLabel := "justification is by faith alone"
  , inferences := [tyndaleStep, tyndaleJamesStep] }

/-! ### The Augsburg Confession, June 1530 -/

/-- **Who draws the step, in June 1530.** The Augsburg Confession, Articles IV
and VI, Triglot pp. 44–47. Rated `disputed`, because the Confutation answers
these very articles, granting Romans 3 and denying faith alone. -/
def augsburgStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work augsburgConfession (.pages 44 45)
  , supporting := [.work augsburgConfession (.pages 46 47)]
  , tradition := .reformedProtestant
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Freely justified through faith, as the Augsburg Confession taught in
1530.** A person is justified by faith apart from works of the law (Romans
3:28), and faith is counted as righteousness to the one who does not work
(4:4–5). So people "are freely justified for Christ's sake, through faith", and,
in the words the sixth article quotes, "without works, by faith alone". -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def augsburgLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "The Augsburg Confession: freely justified through faith (1530)"
  , grounds := [p .romans3_28, p .romans4_4_5]
  , step := ⋀ [p .romans3_28, p .romans4_4_5] ➝ p .justificationByFaithAlone
  , delivers := p .justificationByFaithAlone
  , inference := some augsburgStep }

/-- The Augsburg Confession, as a position. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def augsburgCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  augsburgLine.asPackage reformationCite "justification is by faith alone"

/-! ### The Confutation, August 1530 -/

/-- **Who draws the step to faith formed by love, in August 1530.** The
Confutation, answering the fifth article, p. 212 in Jacobs. Rated `disputed`,
because Luther grants Galatians and denies that the faith which justifies is
formed by love. -/
def confutationFormedStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work romanConfutation (.page 212)
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Who draws the step against faith alone, in August 1530.** The Confutation,
answering the sixth article, pp. 212–213. Rated `disputed`, because the
*Apology* grants James 2 and 1 Corinthians 13 and denies that they exclude faith
alone. -/
def confutationStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work romanConfutation (.pages 212 213)
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Faith that works by love, as the Confutation read Paul in 1530.** "Though
I have all faith, so that I could remove mountains, and have not charity, I am
nothing" (1 Corinthians 13:2, in the Confutation's English), and what counts in
Christ is faith working through love (Galatians 5:6). So the faith that
justifies is "not faith alone, which some incorrectly teach, but faith which
worketh by love". -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def confutationFormedLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "The Confutation: faith which worketh by love (1530)"
  , grounds := [p .galatians5_6, p .firstCorinthians13_2]
  , step := ⋀ [p .galatians5_6, p .firstCorinthians13_2] ➝
      p .justifyingFaithIsFormedByCharity
  , delivers := p .justifyingFaithIsFormedByCharity
  , inference := some confutationFormedStep }

/-- **Faith alone does not justify, as the Confutation answered in 1530.** Faith
without works is dead (James 2:17), and the faith that justifies works by love.
So "their ascription of justification to faith alone is diametrically opposite
the truth of the Gospel, by which works are not excluded", and "St. Paul
certifies to the princes and the entire Church that faith alone does not
justify." -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def confutationLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "The Confutation: faith alone does not justify (1530)"
  , grounds := [p .james2FaithWithoutWorksIsDead]
  , step := ⋀ [p .justifyingFaithIsFormedByCharity, p .james2FaithWithoutWorksIsDead] ➝
      notP .justificationByFaithAlone
  , delivers := notP .justificationByFaithAlone
  , inference := some confutationStep }

/-- The Confutation, as a position: the faith that justifies works by love, so
faith alone does not justify, and works made worthy by grace are
meritorious. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def confutationCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  { name := "The Roman Confutation of the Augsburg Confession (1530)"
  , cite := reformationCite
  , premises := caseOf [confutationFormedLine, confutationLine, meritLine] [] []
  , conclusion := notP .justificationByFaithAlone
  , conclusionLabel := "justification is not by faith alone"
  , inferences := [confutationFormedStep, confutationStep, meritStep] }

/-! ### The *Apology*, 1531 -/

/-- **Who draws the step, in 1531.** Melanchthon's *Apology*, IV.73–78, Triglot
pp. 140–143. Rated `disputed`, because the Confutation grants the texts and
denies the conclusion. -/
def apologyStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work melanchthonApology (.pages 140 143)
  , tradition := .reformedProtestant
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Who reads James that way, in 1531.** The *Apology*, Triglot pp. 188–189.
Rated `disputed`, as Tyndale's reading is, because Eck and the Confutation grant
James 2 and read it as excluding faith alone. -/
def apologyJamesStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work melanchthonApology (.pages 188 189)
  , tradition := .reformedProtestant
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **By faith alone we are justified, as the *Apology* answered in 1531.** "The
particle alone offends some, although even Paul says, Rom. 3, 28", that a man is
justified by faith apart from the works of the law, and we are saved by grace
through faith, "not of works" (Ephesians 2:8–9). The two verses are one argument
only if "faith" means the same in both, and Melanchthon reads it so. What is
excluded is "the opinion of merit", and so "by faith alone we are justified." -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def apologyLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "The Apology: by faith alone we are justified (1531)"
  , grounds := [p .romans3_28, p .ephesians2_8_9, p .faithKeepsItsSenseInRomansAndEphesians]
  , step := ⋀ [p .romans3_28, p .ephesians2_8_9, p .faithKeepsItsSenseInRomansAndEphesians] ➝
      p .justificationByFaithAlone
  , delivers := p .justificationByFaithAlone
  , inference := some apologyStep }

/-- **James does not contradict Paul, as the *Apology* answered in 1531.** The
adversaries "cite 2, 24: Ye see, then, how by works a man is justified, and not
by faith alone", and Melanchthon answers that "if the adversaries do not attach
their own opinions concerning the merits of works, the words of James have in
them nothing that is of disadvantage": James speaks of works that follow a
living faith. So James 2:24 agrees with faith alone. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def apologyJamesLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "The Apology: James does not contradict Paul (1531)"
  , grounds := [p .james2FaithWithoutWorksIsDead, p .james2JustifiedByWorks]
  , step := ⋀ [p .james2FaithWithoutWorksIsDead, p .james2JustifiedByWorks] ➝
      p .james2_24Compatible
  , delivers := p .james2_24Compatible
  , inference := some apologyJamesStep }

/-- The *Apology*, as a position: by faith alone we are justified, and James
does not contradict it. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def apologyCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  { name := "The Apology of the Augsburg Confession (1531)"
  , cite := reformationCite
  , premises := caseOf [apologyLine, apologyJamesLine] [] []
  , conclusion := p .justificationByFaithAlone
  , conclusionLabel := "justification is by faith alone"
  , inferences := [apologyStep, apologyJamesStep] }

/-! ### Barnes's *Supplication*, about 1531 -/

/-- **Who draws the step, about 1531.** Robert Barnes, pp. 226 and 230 of Day's
folio. Rated `disputed`, as Melanchthon's is. -/
def barnesStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work barnesSupplication (.page 226)
  , supporting := [.work barnesSupplication (.page 230)]
  , tradition := .reformedProtestant
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Only faith justifies before God, as Barnes read Paul about 1531.** Paul
preached at Antioch that "by hym are all that beleue, iustified from all
thynges" (Acts 13:39, in Barnes's English), and Barnes read the fathers the same
way: Ambrose on Romans 3, "alonely by fayth are iustified by the gift of God".
So "fayth in Iesus Christ, onely iustifieth afore God." -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def barnesLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Barnes: only faith justifieth before God (c. 1531)"
  , grounds := [p .acts13_39]
  , step := p .acts13_39 ➝ p .justificationByFaithAlone
  , delivers := p .justificationByFaithAlone
  , inference := some barnesStep }

/-- Barnes's article, as a position. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def barnesCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  barnesLine.asPackage reformationCite "justification is by faith alone"

/-! ### Cajetan, 1532 -/

/-- **Who draws the step, in 1532.** Cajetan, *On Faith and Works*, ch. 4.
Rated `disputed`, because Luther, lecturing on Galatians, grants the texts and
denies that the faith which justifies is formed by charity. -/
def cajetanStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work cajetanFaithAndWorks (.sectionRef "ch. 4")
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Justified through faith formed by charity, as Cajetan read Paul in 1532.**
In Galatians 5:6 Paul "clearly ascribes power in Christ not to faith of any kind
but to faith working through love", and without love all faith is nothing
(1 Corinthians 13:2). So "the remission of sins comes not through unformed faith
but through faith formed by charity", and the texts that say we are justified by
faith are understood "of faith formed by divine friendship, which we call
charity". -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def cajetanLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Cajetan: justified through faith formed by charity (1532)"
  , grounds := [p .galatians5_6, p .firstCorinthians13_2]
  , step := ⋀ [p .galatians5_6, p .firstCorinthians13_2] ➝
      p .justifyingFaithIsFormedByCharity
  , delivers := p .justifyingFaithIsFormedByCharity
  , inference := some cajetanStep }

/-- Cajetan's *On Faith and Works*, as a position. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def cajetanCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  cajetanLine.asPackage reformationCite "the faith that justifies is formed by charity"

/-! ### Luther on Galatians, 1535 -/

/-- **Who draws the step, in 1535.** Luther, on Galatians 2:4–5, p. 64 of the
English of 1575 in Blake's edition. Rated `disputed`, because Cajetan and the
Confutation grant Galatians and read 5:6 as faith formed by charity. -/
def lutherGalatiansStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work lutherGalatians1535 (.page 64)
  , tradition := .reformedProtestant
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **The truth of the gospel is faith alone, as Luther read Galatians in
1535.** Paul would not yield to the false brothers "so that the truth of the
gospel would remain" (2:4–5), and a gospel with something added is a different
gospel, under a curse (1:6–9). "The truth of the gospel is, that our
righteousness cometh by faith alone, without the works of the law", and the
false apostles' gospel, "justified by faith, but not without the works of the
law", is what "our Papists" teach when they say that faith "justifieth not,
except it be furnished with charity." So the faith that justifies is not faith
formed by love. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def lutherTruthLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Luther: the truth of the gospel is faith alone (Galatians, 1535)"
  , grounds := [p .galatians2_4_5, p .galatians1_6_9]
  , step := ⋀ [p .galatians2_4_5, p .galatians1_6_9] ➝
      notP .justifyingFaithIsFormedByCharity
  , delivers := notP .justifyingFaithIsFormedByCharity
  , inference := some lutherGalatiansStep }

/-- Luther's 1535 lectures on Galatians 2:4–5, as a position. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def lutherTruthCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  lutherTruthLine.asPackage reformationCite
    "the faith that justifies is not faith formed by love"

/-! ### Sadoleto and Calvin, 1539 -/

/-- **Who draws the step, in March 1539.** Sadoleto, pp. 9–10 in Beveridge.
Rated `disputed`, because Calvin grants the texts and denies that love is any
cause of justification. -/
def sadoletoStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work sadoletoLetterToGenevans (.pages 9 10)
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **In this very faith love is comprehended, as Sadoleto wrote in 1539.**
Christ gave Himself "to prepare a people acceptable to himself, zealous of good
works" (Titus 2:14, in Sadoleto's English). Faith "is a term of full and ample
signification", including "the hope and desire of obeying God, together with
love", so that "when we say, then, that we can be saved by faith alone in God and
Jesus Christ, we hold that in this very faith love is essentially comprehended
as the chief and primary cause of our salvation." Sadoleto says "faith alone"
and means faith formed by love, so his position here is that one. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def sadoletoLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Sadoleto: in this very faith love is comprehended (1539)"
  , grounds := [p .titus2_14]
  , step := p .titus2_14 ➝ p .justifyingFaithIsFormedByCharity
  , delivers := p .justifyingFaithIsFormedByCharity
  , inference := some sadoletoStep }

/-- Sadoleto's letter, as a position. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def sadoletoCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  sadoletoLine.asPackage reformationCite "the faith that justifies is formed by charity"

/-- **Who draws the step, in September 1539.** Calvin, pp. 42–44 in Beveridge.
Rated `disputed`, because Sadoleto grants Paul's texts and holds love the chief
cause of salvation. -/
def calvinSadoletoStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work calvinReplyToSadoleto (.pages 42 43)
  , supporting := [.work calvinReplyToSadoleto (.page 44)]
  , tradition := .reformedProtestant
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Good works have no share in justification, as Calvin answered in
1539.** Paul calls blessed the one to whom God counts righteousness apart from
works, whose sins are covered (Romans 4:6–8). "Faith, you say, is a general
term", but Paul, "whenever he attributes to it the power of justifying, at the
same time restricts it to a gratuitous promise of the divine favour", and so
"we deny that good works have any share in justification, but we claim full
authority for them in the lives of the righteous." So the faith that justifies
is not faith formed by love. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def calvinSadoletoLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Calvin: good works have no share in justification (1539)"
  , grounds := [p .romans4_6_8]
  , step := p .romans4_6_8 ➝ notP .justifyingFaithIsFormedByCharity
  , delivers := notP .justifyingFaithIsFormedByCharity
  , inference := some calvinSadoletoStep }

/-- Calvin's reply to Sadoleto, as a position. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def calvinSadoletoCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  calvinSadoletoLine.asPackage reformationCite
    "the faith that justifies is not faith formed by love"

/-! ### Regensburg, 1541 -/

/-- **Who draws the step to faith efficacious through love, in May 1541.** The
Regensburg article, col. 200, as Contarini glosses it, p. 592. Rated `disputed`,
as Cajetan's is. -/
def regensburgFaithStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work regensburgArticleFive (.sectionRef "col. 200")
  , supporting := [.work contariniOnJustification (.page 592)]
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **Who draws the step to Christ's righteousness, in May 1541.** Contarini,
p. 592, from Philippians 3:8–9, and the Regensburg article, col. 200. Rated
`disputed`, because Trent grants the text six years later and condemns
justification "by the sole imputation of the justice of Christ" (canon 11). -/
def regensburgRestStep : Source :=
  { primary := .work contariniOnJustification (.page 592)
  , supporting := [.work regensburgArticleFive (.sectionRef "col. 200")]
  , tradition := .romanCatholic
  , confidence := .disputed }

/-- **The justifying faith is efficacious through love, as Regensburg agreed in
1541.** What counts in Christ is faith working through love (Galatians 5:6), so
"the justifying faith is that faith which is efficacious through love".
Contarini, who agreed it, explains "efficacious through charity" as "formed by
charity", and the position is read here as he read it. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def regensburgFaithLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Regensburg: justifying faith is efficacious through love (1541)"
  , grounds := [p .galatians5_6]
  , step := p .galatians5_6 ➝ p .justifyingFaithIsFormedByCharity
  , delivers := p .justifyingFaithIsFormedByCharity
  , inference := some regensburgFaithStep }

/-- **The believer rests on Christ's righteousness, as Regensburg and Contarini
held in 1541.** Paul counts all things loss to be found in Christ, "not having a
righteousness of my own", but the righteousness from God through faith
(Philippians 3:8–9). So, though the justified receive an inherent righteousness,
the believer "does not rest on" it, "but on the righteousness of Christ alone,
given to us", for, as Contarini adds, "this righteousness of ours is begun and
imperfect". -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def regensburgRestLine : Line Claim :=
  { name := "Regensburg and Contarini: rest on Christ's righteousness given (1541)"
  , grounds := [p .philippians3_8_9]
  , step := p .philippians3_8_9 ➝ p .restsOnChristsImputedRighteousness
  , delivers := p .restsOnChristsImputedRighteousness
  , inference := some regensburgRestStep }

/-- The Regensburg article, with Contarini's letter, as a position: the faith
that justifies is efficacious through love, and before God the believer rests on
Christ's righteousness given to him. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def regensburgCase : ArgumentPackage Claim :=
  { name := "The Regensburg article on justification (1541)"
  , cite := reformationCite
  , premises := caseOf [regensburgFaithLine, regensburgRestLine] [] []
  , conclusion := p .restsOnChristsImputedRighteousness
  , conclusionLabel := "before God the believer rests on Christ's righteousness given"
  , inferences := [regensburgFaithStep, regensburgRestStep] }

/-! ### The debate, weighed -/

/-- The voices of the Reformation's debate, in the order they wrote. -/
inductive ReformationParty
  /-- Melanchthon's *Loci*, 1521. -/
  | melanchthon
  /-- Zwingli's *Commentary*, 1525. -/
  | zwingli
  /-- Eck's *Enchiridion*, 1525. -/
  | eck
  /-- Tyndale's *Wicked Mammon*, 1528. -/
  | tyndale
  /-- The Augsburg Confession, June 1530. -/
  | augsburg
  /-- The Roman Confutation, August 1530. -/
  | confutation
  /-- The *Apology*, 1531. -/
  | apology
  /-- Barnes's *Supplication*, about 1531. -/
  | barnes
  /-- Cajetan's *On Faith and Works*, 1532. -/
  | cajetan
  /-- Luther on Galatians, 1535. -/
  | luther
  /-- Sadoleto's letter to the Genevans, March 1539. -/
  | sadoleto
  /-- Calvin's reply to Sadoleto, September 1539. -/
  | calvin
  /-- The Regensburg article, with Contarini's letter, May 1541. -/
  | regensburg
deriving DecidableEq

/-- The package each voice argues from. -/
@[solaFideDefs]
def reformationNode : ReformationParty → ArgumentPackage Claim
  | .melanchthon => melanchthonCase
  | .zwingli => zwingliCase
  | .eck => eckCase
  | .tyndale => tyndaleCase
  | .augsburg => augsburgCase
  | .confutation => confutationCase
  | .apology => apologyCase
  | .barnes => barnesCase
  | .cajetan => cajetanCase
  | .luther => lutherTruthCase
  | .sadoleto => sadoletoCase
  | .calvin => calvinSadoletoCase
  | .regensburg => regensburgCase

/-- The Reformation's debate on justification, 1521–1541. -/
declare_dispute reformationDispute := reformationNode using solaFideDefs
  /-- **Who defeats whom.** Every defeat crosses from one side to the other, and
  every one is returned, because every step on both sides is rated `disputed`.

  On faith alone, Eck and the Confutation meet each of the five voices who
  teach it: Melanchthon, Tyndale, the Augsburg Confession, the *Apology* and
  Barnes. On merit, Eck and the Confutation meet Zwingli. On faith formed by
  love, Luther and Calvin meet each of the four voices who teach it: the
  Confutation, Cajetan, Sadoleto and Regensburg. Nothing on one side defeats
  anything on its own side. Every cell is checked by the kernel. -/
  defeats [ (.melanchthon, .confutation), (.confutation, .melanchthon)
          , (.tyndale, .confutation), (.confutation, .tyndale)
          , (.augsburg, .confutation), (.confutation, .augsburg)
          , (.apology, .confutation), (.confutation, .apology)
          , (.barnes, .confutation), (.confutation, .barnes)
          , (.luther, .confutation), (.confutation, .luther)
          , (.melanchthon, .eck), (.eck, .melanchthon), (.tyndale, .eck), (.eck, .tyndale)
          , (.augsburg, .eck), (.eck, .augsburg), (.apology, .eck), (.eck, .apology)
          , (.barnes, .eck), (.eck, .barnes)
          , (.luther, .cajetan), (.cajetan, .luther), (.luther, .sadoleto), (.sadoleto, .luther)
          , (.luther, .regensburg), (.regensburg, .luther)
          , (.calvin, .confutation), (.confutation, .calvin), (.calvin, .cajetan)
          , (.cajetan, .calvin), (.calvin, .sadoleto), (.sadoleto, .calvin)
          , (.calvin, .regensburg), (.regensburg, .calvin)
          , (.zwingli, .eck), (.eck, .zwingli), (.zwingli, .confutation), (.confutation, .zwingli) ]
  /-- **Nothing supports anything.** Every cell is checked by the kernel. -/
  supports []
  /-- **Cajetan's case is part of the Confutation's**, which argues from the same
  two texts to the same faith formed by love, and goes on from there. Every case
  contains itself. Nothing else. Every cell is checked by the kernel. -/
  partOf [(.cajetan, .confutation)]
  /-- The Reformation's debate drawn: who defeats whom. -/
  map reformationMap

/-- **The Reformation's case, told in the order it was made.** Each turn is a
voice of the years before Trent, named with the work it comes from and that
work's date, and the turns come in the order of those dates. Where a turn is
said to meet an earlier one, the two defeat each other, as the solver computes
from their premises. -/
declare_bundle reformationExchange in reformationDispute.finite where
  /-- In December 1521 Melanchthon's *Loci* teaches, from Abraham and Romans 4,
  that "faith alone justifies". -/
  turn ReformationParty.melanchthon from melanchthonLoci
  /-- In March 1525 Zwingli argues from Galatians 2:16 and Romans 4:4 and 11:6
  that grace "is of no avail if salvation be due to works". -/
  turn ReformationParty.zwingli from zwingliTrueAndFalseReligion
  /-- The same year Eck's *Enchiridion* answers the Lutherans from James 2 and
  Galatians 5:6: "faith does not suffice without works", and works, made worthy
  by grace, merit eternal life. -/
  turn ReformationParty.eck meets ReformationParty.melanchthon from eckEnchiridion
  /-- In 1528 Tyndale teaches that "faith only justifieth" before God, from Romans
  1:16–17, and that the deeds of James 2 "justify outwardly before the world". -/
  turn ReformationParty.tyndale meets ReformationParty.eck from tyndaleWickedMammon
  /-- In June 1530 the Augsburg Confession confesses that people "are freely
  justified for Christ's sake, through faith", citing Romans 3 and 4. -/
  turn ReformationParty.augsburg from augsburgConfession
  /-- In August 1530 the Confutation answers that "faith alone does not justify",
  from 1 Corinthians 13:2, James 2:17 and Galatians 5:6, and that "it is entirely
  contrary to Holy Scripture to deny that our works are meritorious". -/
  turn ReformationParty.confutation meets ReformationParty.augsburg from romanConfutation
  /-- In 1531 the *Apology* answers it from Romans 3:28 and Ephesians 2:8–9,
  "therefore by faith alone we are justified", and reads James 2:24 as
  agreeing. -/
  turn ReformationParty.apology meets ReformationParty.confutation from melanchthonApology
  /-- About 1531 Barnes, in England, argues from Acts 13:39 and the fathers that
  "onely fayth iustifieth before God". -/
  turn ReformationParty.barnes from barnesSupplication
  /-- In 1532 Cajetan holds that "the remission of sins comes not through
  unformed faith but through faith formed by charity", from Galatians 5:6. -/
  turn ReformationParty.cajetan from cajetanFaithAndWorks
  /-- In 1535 Luther, on Galatians 2:4–5, calls the teaching that faith
  "justifieth not, except it be furnished with charity" the false apostles'
  corruption of the gospel. -/
  turn ReformationParty.luther meets ReformationParty.cajetan from lutherGalatians1535
  /-- In March 1539 Sadoleto writes to Geneva that we are saved "by faith alone",
  but by a faith in which "love is essentially comprehended as the chief and
  primary cause of our salvation". -/
  turn ReformationParty.sadoleto meets ReformationParty.luther from sadoletoLetterToGenevans
  /-- In September 1539 Calvin answers from Romans 4:6–8 that "good works have
  [no] share in justification", and that Paul restricts justifying faith "to a
  gratuitous promise of the divine favour". -/
  turn ReformationParty.calvin meets ReformationParty.sadoleto from calvinReplyToSadoleto
  /-- In May 1541 the Regensburg article, with Contarini's letter, holds both:
  the faith that justifies is "efficacious through love", and the believer rests
  "on the righteousness of Christ alone, given to us". Read as Contarini read
  it, its first half meets Calvin as Sadoleto's did. -/
  turn ReformationParty.regensburg meets ReformationParty.calvin from regensburgArticleFive

/-- The Reformers' reading, on which every one of their positions holds: faith
alone justifies, the faith that justifies is not formed by love, no work is a
ground of salvation, and James agrees with Paul. Where it is otherwise silent it
holds every claim, except those the argument's meanings exclude. -/
def reformersReading : Valuation Claim := fun a =>
  match a with
  | .justifyingFaithIsFormedByCharity => False
  | .justificationIsForensicOnly => False
  | .baptismIsInstrumentalCause => False
  | .jamesUsesDikaioAsPaul => False
  | .rev22_11BeJustifiedStill => False
  | .sir18_22BeJustifiedToDeath => False
  | _ => True

/-- **The Reformers' positions stand together.** One reading satisfies
Melanchthon, Zwingli, Tyndale, the Augsburg Confession, the *Apology*, Barnes,
Luther and Calvin at once: whatever each argues from, none of them denies what
another holds. -/
theorem the_reformers_stand_together :
    reformationDispute.StandTogether
      [.melanchthon, .zwingli, .tyndale, .augsburg, .apology, .barnes, .luther, .calvin] := by
  satisfied_by reformersReading [Dispute.StandTogether, solaFideDefs]

/-- The Catholic reading of the same years, on which every Catholic position
holds, Regensburg's among them: faith alone does not justify, the faith that
justifies is formed by love, good works made worthy by grace are a ground of
salvation, and before God the believer rests on Christ's righteousness given.
Where it is otherwise silent it holds every claim, except those the argument's
meanings exclude. -/
def romeAndRegensburgReading : Valuation Claim := fun a =>
  match a with
  | .justificationByFaithAlone => False
  | .salvationNotByWorks => False
  | .justificationIsForensicOnly => False
  | .baptismIsInstrumentalCause => False
  | .jamesUsesDikaioAsPaul => False
  | .rev22_11BeJustifiedStill => False
  | .sir18_22BeJustifiedToDeath => False
  | _ => True

/-- **The Catholic positions stand together, and Regensburg with them.** One
reading satisfies Eck, the Confutation, Cajetan, Sadoleto and the Regensburg
article at once, because the article's faith "efficacious through love" is read
as Contarini read it, faith formed by love, and its resting on Christ's
righteousness given denies nothing the others hold. -/
theorem rome_and_regensburg_stand_together :
    reformationDispute.StandTogether [.eck, .confutation, .cajetan, .sadoleto, .regensburg] := by
  satisfied_by romeAndRegensburgReading [Dispute.StandTogether, solaFideDefs]

/-- Why nothing is forced: every voice is defeated by one from the other side,
and defeats it in return. -/
declare_verdict reformationSettles for reformationDispute :
    .nothingGrounded
      [ (.zwingli, .eck), (.melanchthon, .confutation), (.tyndale, .confutation)
      , (.augsburg, .confutation), (.apology, .confutation), (.barnes, .confutation)
      , (.luther, .confutation), (.confutation, .melanchthon), (.eck, .melanchthon)
      , (.cajetan, .luther), (.sadoleto, .luther), (.calvin, .sadoleto)
      , (.regensburg, .luther) ]
  /-- **Before Trent, the Reformation's debate forces nothing.** Heard together,
  every voice from 1521 to 1541 is defeated by one from the other side and
  defeats it in return, because every step on both sides is rated `disputed`:
  each side grants the other's texts and denies its conclusion. So no position
  is forced, and none is ruled out. The sides part at two claims: whether the
  faith that justifies does so alone or as formed by love, and whether good
  works made worthy by grace are a ground of salvation. -/
  proves @[headline] luthers_contemporaries_force_nothing

#print axioms luthers_contemporaries_force_nothing

end TestimonyArguments.SolaFide
